 "modules": [
  {
   "id": "il01-hermetic-foundations",
   "code": "IL-01",
   "title": "Hermetic Foundations",
   "summary": "Welcome to the Interstitium Labs Esoteric Foundations academy — IL-01. I’m your instructor for this academy, and over the next three modules we’re going to read the Western esoteric current the way historians read anything: primary sources first, every claim tiered honestly, and belief never required. This is Module 1: Hermetic Foundations — the current from Alexandria to the present: the Hermetic Corpus (Corpus Hermeticum, Asclepius, Nag Hammadi context, late-antique Alexandria), the Golden Dawn (1888–1903 order history, grade system, the LBRP as study object), Wicca and Thelema (Gardnerian origins, Crowley/A.’.’A.’./O.T.O., the Book of the Law), chaos magic and Discordianism (Spare’s sigilization, Carroll’s paradigm shifting, the Principia), Hermetic Qabalah (the Tree of Life, correspondences, tarot), and alchemy (Alexandrian roots, Paracelsus, laboratory vs. spiritual readings) — every text read through the four epistemic tiers: DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, CONTESTED.",
   "objectives": [
    "Tier claims: DOCUMENTED vs SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS vs TRADITIONAL vs CONTESTED.",
    "Read the Hermetic Corpus in its Alexandrian context.",
    "Trace the Golden Dawn's papers: what they actually say.",
    "Compare laboratory and spiritual readings of alchemy."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Corpus Hermeticum** — the dialogues attributed to Hermes Trismegistus; late-antique Alexandrian texts, not primeval Egyptian scripture.\n- **Asclepius** — the most complete single Hermetic text; read it first.\n- **Nag Hammadi** — the 1945 find that reframed the world the Hermetic texts belong to.\n- **Late-antique Alexandria** — the syncretic city where Greek philosophy, Egyptian religion, and emerging Christianity met; the Corpus is what happens when they share a city.\n- **Casaubon (1614)** — dated the Corpus to late antiquity by philology; the founding act of esoteric historiography. Texts have dates, and dates have consequences.\n- **Cipher Manuscripts** — the Golden Dawn’s founding documents: DOCUMENTED as artifacts, CONTESTED as to origin; tier each claim separately.\n- **Golden Dawn (1888–1903)** — the order of Westcott, Mathers, and Woodman, through the schisms; read its grade papers as a curriculum, not a mystery.\n- **Grade system** — the ladder from 0=0 upward; a syllabus: what’s taught, in what order, to what end.\n- **LBRP** — the Lesser Banishing Ritual of the Pentagram; a Golden Dawn paper and our study object. Note what the papers say vs. what later books claim.\n- **Gardnerian Wicca** — Wicca’s documented origins; tier the origin claims as you meet them.\n- **Thelema** — Crowley’s current: A.’.’A.’., O.T.O., and the Book of the Law.\n- **Book of the Law** — primary text first, Crowley’s commentaries second, others’ opinions last.\n- **Sigilization** — Austin Osman Spare’s technique: DOCUMENTED as technique, CONTESTED as mechanism.\n- **Paradigm shifting** — Peter Carroll’s chaos-magical move: belief as instrument, a tool rather than a truth-claim.\n- **Principia Discordia** — the Discordian scripture; chaos magic’s irreverent cousin.\n- **Tree of Life** — the Hermetic Qabalah’s map; a memory palace organizing planets, elements, and tarot, whatever else it is.\n- **Correspondences** — the Qabalistic system of linkages across planets, elements, and tarot.\n- **Paracelsus** — iatrochemistry: alchemy as early chemistry and medicine; “the dose makes the poison” is alchemy’s most DOCUMENTED contribution to science.\n- **DOCUMENTED** — primary sources, artifacts, dated texts: the bedrock tier.\n- **SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS** — what historians agree on: provisional, cited.\n- **TRADITIONAL** — what lineages claim about themselves: reported, not endorsed.\n- **CONTESTED** — open questions and rival readings: where the seminar lives."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Before we theorize, we practice the one reflex this academy exists to build: tiering a claim. You’ll use the Tiering Engine lab (il01-lab-tier) — but the procedure is identical on paper.\n\n1. **Choose one claim** from this module’s readings — for example, “The Corpus Hermeticum preserves primeval Egyptian wisdom.”\n2. **Split it into atomic sub-claims:** (a) the manuscripts exist and date to late antiquity; (b) their content originates in primeval Egypt.\n3. **Collect the evidence you actually hold** for each sub-claim: the manuscript tradition and Casaubon’s 1614 philology for (a); lineage assertion for (b).\n4. **Run each sub-claim** through the lab’s tier_claim function with its evidence string, and read the (tier, justification) pairs it returns.\n5. **Record every pair in your tiered reading journal** with the evidence cited — the justification matters more than the label.\n6. **Circle the CONTESTED sub-claims.** Those are not failures; they are your seminar questions for the rest of the academy."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "The brief. Choose one text from this module — the Asclepius, a Golden Dawn grade paper, or a passage of the Book of the Law — and produce a tiered dossier a historian would respect.\n\nDeliverables. (1) A claim inventory: every factual claim the text or its surrounding tradition makes about origins, authorship, and transmission. (2) A tier for each claim — DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED — with the evidence cited. (3) One CONTESTED question argued both ways, about 300 words per side. (4) A reading-order recommendation for the next student: what to read first, and why.\n\nThe standard. Your tiers must survive cross-examination: TRADITIONAL is never marked wrong for being traditional, and DOCUMENTED is never awarded without evidence. Assessments test whether your dossier holds up — never what you believe."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Original study material, written for this academy — the questions, labs, and scenarios here are authored, not transcribed from any order’s papers. The four tier labels are our historiographic method, not verdicts on the traditions: TRADITIONAL means “the lineage claims this,” reported accurately and labeled honestly — never endorsed, never mocked. “The lineage claims X” is itself a DOCUMENTED fact about the claim, whatever X’s own tier is. Interstitium Labs is not affiliated with any esoteric order and confers no credential; assessments test literacy — what the texts say, what tier applies, how scholars argue — never belief."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: A text claims primeval Egyptian origins; philology dates it to 200…",
     "body": "**Problem.** A text claims primeval Egyptian origins; philology dates it to 200 CE. Tier?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** CONTESTED on origins; DOCUMENTED on the manuscript date\n- **Why:** Tier each claim separately — the manuscript’s date is DOCUMENTED; the origin story is CONTESTED. (Concepts: the four tiers; Hands-on procedure, step 2.)\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Tier each claim separately — the manuscript’s date is DOCUMENTED; the origin story is CONTESTED.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Esoteric material punishes sloppy epistemics harder than most subjects. These are the failure modes that separate scholars from storytellers in Hermetic Foundations:\n- **Collapsing the tiers.** The single most common failure: treating a TRADITIONAL claim as DOCUMENTED because it feels old. Every claim you touch in Hermetic Foundations gets an explicit tier — DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED — before you use it.\n- **Anachronism.** Reading modern meanings back into period terms. When a Hermetic Foundations source says 'spirit', 'energy', or 'science', find what the word meant to the author, not what it means on social media.\n- **Single-source trust.** One grimoire, one translation, one blogger is not a consensus. Corroborate across at least two independent sources before a claim leaves your notes.\n- **Belief creep.** Studying a system is not joining it. Keep the scholar's bracket: you can map a cosmology precisely while suspending assent. Losing the bracket turns analysis into apologetics.\n- **Etymology as evidence.** Word origins are interesting and prove almost nothing about historical claims. 'The word X comes from Y' is a linguistic fact, not a historical argument about Hermetic Foundations.\n- **Skipping the primary source.** Summaries of summaries drift. Read the actual passage — even in translation — before you argue about what a Hermetic Foundations text says."
    }
   ],
