  {
   "id": "il01-dee-enochian",
   "code": "IL-01",
   "title": "Dee and the Enochian System",
   "summary": "Welcome back to IL-01 — Esoteric Foundations. I’m your instructor, and in Module 2 we work a single life’s paper trail: John Dee’s. This is Module 2: Dee and the Enochian System — Dee’s documented life (1527–1608/09: court mathematician, navigator, advisor; the Mortlake library), the Enochian alphabet (21 letters, received 6 May 1583), the Angelical Keys (the 48/49 Calls, the 1581/82–1589 diaries, Kelly and Dee’s scrying partnership), the Monas Hieroglyphica (1564: the glyph as a synthesis of geometry, alchemy, and astronomy), and the scholarly reception (Casaubon’s True and Faithful Relation, Yates and the modern recovery, current work, the open questions). Praxis here means literacy: the commonplace book, the tiered reading journal — no belief required.",
   "objectives": [
    "Narrate Dee's documented biography: mathematician, navigator, advisor.",
    "Describe the Enochian system's documented components: alphabet, Calls, diaries.",
    "Read the Monas Hieroglyphica as a synthetic glyph.",
    "Summarize the scholarly reception: Casaubon, Yates, current work."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **John Dee (1527–1608/09)** — court mathematician, navigator, and advisor; the biographical record is DOCUMENTED throughout.\n- **‘Brittany’** — Dee’s imperial-imagination concept; the British Empire idea carries his fingerprints.\n- **Mortlake** — Dee’s library, the largest in England; his power was bibliographic before it was anything else.\n- **Enochian alphabet** — 21 letters, received 6 May 1583; the date is DOCUMENTED, the reception narrative TRADITIONAL.\n- **Angelical Keys** — the 48 (or 49) Calls; study their structure — the grammar claims — before their content.\n- **The diaries (1581/82–1589)** — the day-by-day record; genuine 16th-century documents by SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS.\n- **Kelly** — the scryer; Dee recorded. The division of labor shapes how the diaries must be read.\n- **Scrying** — the working practice of the partnership; the diaries are its logbook.\n- **Monas Hieroglyphica (1564)** — the glyph synthesizing geometry, alchemy, and astronomy; read it as a diagram that thinks.\n- **True and Faithful Relation (1659)** — Casaubon’s hostile edition of the diaries; the attack that preserved them.\n- **Hostile transmission** — Casaubon’s irony: the debunker preserved what he attacked; hostile transmission is still transmission.\n- **Yates** — the 20th-century recovery of Dee; the ‘Hermetic tradition’ thesis, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS with live CONTESTED edges.\n- **Commonplace book** — the early-modern method: copy the striking passage, note the tier, add your question.\n- **Tiered reading journal** — every session logged with what you read, what tier it is, and the evidence; the assessment artifact."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Dee’s diaries reward the slow reader. This procedure turns one dated entry into a tiered journal session — the same move you’ll repeat across the corpus.\n\n1. **Select one dated entry** from the 1581/82–1589 diaries — a short one; the boring entries are often the most DOCUMENTED.\n2. **Copy the entry into your commonplace book** verbatim, with its date and archive reference.\n3. **List the entry’s atomic claims:** who was present, what was seen or received, what was recorded.\n4. **Tier each claim separately:** the entry’s existence and date are DOCUMENTED; the reception narrative is TRADITIONAL; interpretations of what happened are CONTESTED.\n5. **Note the division of labor** in this entry — what Kelly saw versus what Dee wrote — and how it shapes your reading.\n6. **Write one open question** the entry leaves you with, and one Monas theorem or passage it echoes, if any.\n7. **Log the session** in your tiered journal: claim, tier, evidence, question. Coverage beats cherry-picking — read the boring parts."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "The brief. Build the Dee dossier — three parts, one standard: the document is tiered separately from its content.\n\nDeliverables. (1) A documented timeline of Dee’s life — mathematician, navigator, advisor, Mortlake — with a source for every event: letters, library catalogues, dated works. (2) A tiered reading of one Monas theorem or one Angelical Key: structure first (what the figure or the Call’s grammar claims), content second, every claim tiered. (3) A reception comparison: Casaubon’s 1659 True and Faithful Relation against Yates’s 20th-century recovery — what each preserves, what each argues, and what tier each of their claims earns.\n\nThe standard. The diaries’ genuineness is SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS; what they record is the open question — tier the document separately from its content, or the dossier fails. No belief is required or tested; precision is."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Dee the mathematician and Dee the magus are the same man — the split is a modern invention, and this module refuses it. ‘Received 6 May 1583’ is a DOCUMENTED date from the record; the reception narrative around it is TRADITIONAL; what the alphabet is remains CONTESTED — we keep those three apart at all times. The diaries are genuine 16th-century documents; genuineness is a fact about the paper, not about the angels. Original study material throughout; Interstitium Labs is affiliated with no order and confers no credential; assessments test whether you can read, tier, and argue — never what you affirm."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Dee's imperial concept?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Dee's imperial concept?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** 'Brittany' — the British Empire idea\n- **Why:** Dee advised on the imperial imagination — DOCUMENTED advisory role. (Concepts: ‘Brittany’.)\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Dee advised on the imperial imagination — DOCUMENTED advisory role.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Esoteric material punishes sloppy epistemics harder than most subjects. These are the failure modes that separate scholars from storytellers in Dee and the Enochian System:\n- **Collapsing the tiers.** The single most common failure: treating a TRADITIONAL claim as DOCUMENTED because it feels old. Every claim you touch in Dee and the Enochian System gets an explicit tier — DOCUMENTED, SCHOLARLY CONSENSUS, TRADITIONAL, or CONTESTED — before you use it.\n- **Anachronism.** Reading modern meanings back into period terms. When a Dee and the Enochian System source says 'spirit', 'energy', or 'science', find what the word meant to the author, not what it means on social media.\n- **Single-source trust.** One grimoire, one translation, one blogger is not a consensus. Corroborate across at least two independent sources before a claim leaves your notes.\n- **Belief creep.** Studying a system is not joining it. Keep the scholar's bracket: you can map a cosmology precisely while suspending assent. Losing the bracket turns analysis into apologetics.\n- **Etymology as evidence.** Word origins are interesting and prove almost nothing about historical claims. 'The word X comes from Y' is a linguistic fact, not a historical argument about Dee and the Enochian System.\n- **Skipping the primary source.** Summaries of summaries drift. Read the actual passage — even in translation — before you argue about what a Dee and the Enochian System text says."
    }
   ],
