  {
   "id": "il01-ccru-seminar",
   "code": "IL-01",
   "title": "The CCRU Seminar: Hyperstition and Cybernetics",
   "summary": "Welcome to Module 3 — the seminar. I’m your instructor, and for this final module we change register: from the archive to the argument. This is Module 3: The CCRU Seminar — Nick Land and the CCRU (the Warwick 1990s collective: who, where, what; the writings; primary texts in reading order; the archive itself as artifact), hyperstition (fiction as an engine, the hyperstitional circuit, examples and counter-examples — a CONTESTED theory, labeled as such), the numogram and syzygies (decimal sorcery, working the diagram, reading a technical diagram as a text), meme chaos-magic theory (Spare’s sigilization in the network age, memes as hyperstitional objects, attention as a medium), and Project Cybersyn (Allende’s Chile 1971–73, Stafford Beer and the viable system model, the operations room — what was actually built versus what has been mythologized). Read Land’s 1990s corpus, then argue about what it means — with tiers.",
   "objectives": [
    "Map the CCRU: people, place, texts, in reading order.",
    "Define hyperstition and test it against examples.",
    "Work the numogram as a technical diagram.",
    "Separate Cybersyn's documented build from its mythology."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **CCRU** — the Cybernetic Culture Research Unit, Warwick University, mid-to-late 1990s: the collective, the texts, the legend.\n- **Nick Land** — the 1990s writings are the seminar’s spine; the later work is a separate question, out of scope.\n- **Hyperstition** — fiction as an engine: ideas that make themselves real by being believed and circulated.\n- **Hyperstitional circuit** — belief → action → reality → belief; the feedback loop is the theory’s signature.\n- **Theory status** — CONTESTED: provocative framework or mystified feedback loop is the live debate.\n- **Numogram** — the decimal-sorcery diagram; treat it like an engineering drawing — precise about an imprecise thing.\n- **Syzygies** — the numogram’s currents and pairings; work the diagram, don’t just admire it.\n- **Meme chaos-magic** — Spare’s sigilization, networked: memes as hyperstitional objects at internet scale.\n- **Attention as a medium** — the economics of the circuit: what the loop runs on.\n- **Stafford Beer** — management cybernetics; the serious theory under the Cybersyn legend.\n- **Viable system model (VSM)** — Beer’s model; study it before buying the chairs.\n- **Project Cybersyn** — Allende’s Chile, 1971–1973: the telex network, the VSM design, the operations room.\n- **Mythologized Cybersyn** — the ‘internet before the internet’ claims; tier the hype separately from the build.\n- **The archive as artifact** — how the CCRU texts survived (websites, samizdat, reissues) is part of the story; web-era texts vanish."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Two exercises, one discipline: separate what was built from what was mythologized, and read a diagram as data.\n\n1. **List the Cybersyn claims:** the telex network, the VSM design, the operations room with its chairs and screens, and the ‘internet before the internet’ gloss.\n2. **Sort them:** DOCUMENTED build (telex network, VSM design, ops room, 1971–73) versus mythologized legacy (the gloss) — tier the hype separately.\n3. **Run the Numogram Walker lab** (il01-lab-numogram): build the digit graph from the syzygy pairings, BFS from 0, and confirm connectivity — the diagram as data you can walk.\n4. **Take one hyperstition candidate** from the corpus and test it against the circuit: did it change behavior before it was ‘true’? Record the counter-examples too — failed prophecies are data.\n5. **Journal the session:** every claim tiered, every tier evidenced. The circuit needs the feedback to close; check whether it did."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "The brief. Choose one candidate hyperstition — from the Land/CCRU corpus or the meme layer — and build the case file.\n\nDeliverables. (1) The circuit, documented: belief → action → reality → belief, with dated evidence at each arrow — or the dated evidence that the loop failed to close. (2) Counter-examples: at least two failed prophecies or stillborn circuits, tiered. (3) A verdict of about 500 words argued both ways: the strongest case that the circuit is real, and the strongest case that it is mystified feedback. (4) The Cybersyn contrast: one page on what honest tiering looks like when applied to a documented build versus its mythology.\n\nThe standard. The theory’s CONTESTED status is not a defect to hide — it is the seminar’s subject. You are graded on whether your tiers hold and your evidence is dated, never on which side you take."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Hyperstition is presented as a CONTESTED theory throughout — the seminar’s job is to map the argument, not to settle it. Land’s post-1990s work is a separate question and out of scope; we read the 1990s corpus. Cybersyn’s documented build (telex network, VSM design, ops room, 1971–73) and its mythologized legacy are different tiers, and we never let the legend borrow the build’s credibility. Meme chaos-magic is taught as hypothesis — the chaos-magic lineage is real, the network-age extension is argued, not asserted. Original study material; no credential conferred; assessments test whether you can map the circuit and tier the claims — never what you believe."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Hyperstitional circuit?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Hyperstitional circuit?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** belief→action→reality→belief\n- **Why:** The feedback loop — ideas bootstrapping themselves into reality. (Concepts: hyperstitional circuit.)\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *The feedback loop — ideas bootstrapping themselves into reality.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
